# Generate the real file next to this one:
#
#   docker run --rm httpd:alpine htpasswd -nbB matt 'a-long-password' > config/.htpasswd
#
# -B is bcrypt. Do NOT use -n without -B; nginx accepts the weaker formats and
# this file is what stands between the internet and your campaign records.
#
# config/.htpasswd is gitignored.

# One more thing, and it is the one that bites: nginx's WORKERS run as uid 101
# inside the container, and `htpasswd >` writes 600 owned by you. nginx then
# cannot read its own password file, and every request that should have got a
# password prompt gets 500 instead — which looks like the site being broken
# rather than like a permissions problem, and only shows up for people the
# allow-list does not cover, so it can sit there unnoticed for months.
#
#   chmod 644 config/.htpasswd
#
# It is a bcrypt hash, not a password.

